From a29187a6fb6989a060d0628d7bf65e7adb36f4b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nick Gamb <nick.gamb@solo.io>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 14:02:32 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix(ateom-gvisor): actor networking that works under
 in-pod traffic capture

Under a CNI or service mesh that captures traffic inside the pod netns
(Istio ambient, for one), actors never become ready and never serve:

- ateom's own connections to the actor (readyz) are locally generated, so
  the mesh's OUTPUT redirect captures them and they never reach the veth.
- Inbound traffic to the worker's :80 is DNAT'd in PREROUTING to the actor
  veth, racing the mesh's own inbound redirect at the same hook. Whichever
  wins, a meshed caller loses: the DNAT bypasses mTLS and authorization, and
  a mesh that terminates the connection delivers it to a local socket on
  :80 where nothing listens.

This change:

- relays inbound connections to :80 from a userspace listener in ateom to
  the actor, instead of the PREROUTING DNAT. Inbound traffic ends on a local
  socket like any pod's, so whatever terminates or authorizes it first keeps
  working. Without a mesh the path is the same as before, minus the NAT.
- pins worker-local connections to the actor veth IP with a no-op DNAT in
  ateom's table at priority dstnat-50. The kernel applies only the first NAT
  binding a connection gets, so later redirects (the mesh's) never see them.

Actor egress is unchanged (masquerade). A mesh can take it over per pod,
e.g. Istio's istio.io/reroute-virtual-interfaces: ateom0, which the new
WorkerPool template annotations can set.
